// Coming Of Age: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== coming-of-age.f01 | feature | journey
title: The Long Way To Oban
genre: coming-of-age + road-movie
tone: Salt-aired, funny and unguarded; a summer that knows it is ending
era: 1996 | period
setting: The west coast of Scotland, from Glasgow to Oban | real | road
logline: In the summer of 1996 a fifteen-year-old boy who has just learned he is adopted steals his grandfather's milk float to deliver a letter to the woman he thinks is his mother, at eight miles an hour.
premise: {callum} finds the letter in a biscuit tin the week his grandfather {archie} retires from the dairy: a woman in Oban, writing every birthday for fifteen years, never answered. Oban is ninety-four miles away. {callum} cannot drive, but he has ridden the electric float on {archie}'s round since he was six. Its range is thirty miles on a charge. His best friend {priya} comes because somebody has to read the map, and because she is leaving for Leicester in September and hasn't told him.
synopsis: The film is a journey in four charges of a battery. In Glasgow, {callum} is the boy who knows every doorstep on the round and nothing about himself. The tin, the letters and a photograph of a girl of seventeen on a ferry undo that in an afternoon. He takes the float at four in the morning with a crate of sterilised milk for ballast and {priya} in the passenger seat with an Ordnance Survey map and a Walkman. The first charge gets them to Dumbarton, where they sleep in a bowling-club shed and plug into its mains. {archie} wakes to an empty depot, tells nobody, and follows on the service bus with the charger's adaptor in a carrier bag, because he knows what the boy forgot. The road is the middle of the film: Loch Lomond in rain at walking pace with a queue of caravans behind them; a farmer, {morag}, who trades a night's charge for a morning's milking and tells {callum}, unprompted, what it is to give a child up; a ceilidh in Inveraray where {priya} dances with someone else and {callum} discovers that jealousy and grief feel the same in the chest. She tells him about Leicester on the Rest And Be Thankful, the float crawling up the pass at three miles an hour, and he says the worst thing he can think of and she gets out and walks, faster than the float, for a mile. {archie} catches them at Inveraray. The scene the film has been waiting for is not with the mother: it is the old man and the boy on a harbour wall, and {archie} saying that he kept the letters because he could not bear to answer and could not bear to burn them, and that both of those were about him. He hands over the adaptor. He does not ask {callum} to come home. In Oban, on the last of the fourth charge, the address is a guest house. The woman, {eilidh}, opens the door with a tea towel in her hand, sees the float before she sees him, and knows the dairy's name. She is not what he imagined and she is exactly the girl in the photograph. They have one afternoon, most of it spent not saying the large things: she shows him how to bleed a radiator. He gives her the letter he wrote back, which is the one he set out to deliver. She has a husband and two small daughters due home at five. He leaves at half past four, by his own choice. On the way out of town the battery dies on the esplanade. {archie} and {priya} push. {callum} steers.
question: When you find out where you came from, do you have to go and live there?
themes: belonging chosen over belonging found; slowness as a way of arriving ready; grandfathers
audience: Teenagers and families; adults who were fifteen in a summer like it
viewpoint: With {callum}; with {archie} alone on buses, two beats behind
stakes: {callum}'s idea of who he is, his place in the only home he has, a friendship with an end date, and an old man's fear that the letters were the last thing holding the boy to him
conflict: {callum}'s need to be claimed by a stranger against the people who have already claimed him, and against a vehicle that will not be hurried
ending: He meets her, likes her, and leaves before her family comes home. The float dies in sight of the ferry. His grandfather and his friend push it and he steers. September comes.
experience: Laughing at eight miles an hour, a lump in the throat on a harbour wall, and leaving the cinema wanting to ring someone old
look: Super 16 grain, wet greens and tarmac; the float white and absurd against big hills; long lenses on the queue of traffic behind
sound: The float's electric whine, bottles ticking in crates, a compilation tape of 1996 that gets chewed halfway. Fiddle only at the ceilidh.
m: find | self | belonging-vs-identity | reconciled
@ callum | Callum Reid | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 15 | To put a letter in his mother's hand | He has just learned that everyone knew but him | Ninety-four miles, a thirty-mile battery, and what he will do if she is kind | Capable with his hands and reckless with his mouth | From demanding to be claimed to choosing who to go home with | Glaswegian, quick, funnier than he means to be when scared | Dairy jacket two sizes too big, Britpop fringe
@ priya | Priya Chaudhry | co-lead | deuteragonist | human | she/her | 15 | One whole summer before she goes | Her family is moving for her father's job | She hasn't told him and every mile makes it harder | Organised, wry, braver than him and less noisy about it | From keeping it from him to making him hear it | Dry, precise, sings harmonies under her breath | Map case round her neck, Walkman, her brother's cagoule
@ archie | Archie Reid | supporting | mentor | human | he/him | 71 | The boy home, or at least not alone | He kept fifteen years of letters in a tin and answered none | He is the one who has to explain it | Steady as a doorstep, frightened of one conversation | From hiding the letters to handing over the charger | Few words, weather, and the names of customers | Flat cap, dairy overall under his good coat, a carrier bag
@ eilidh | Eilidh MacInnes | supporting | none | human | she/her | 32 | To know he was all right | She was seventeen and it was decided for her | A life with no room made in it for this | Warm, practical, guarded by habit | From a photograph to a person with a tea towel | Soft West Highland, deflects with tasks | Guest-house apron, hair tied up with a pencil
@ morag | Morag Lamont | featured | ally | human | she/her | 58 | A morning's help and no fuss | She has a story of her own that she tells once | None of her business, which has never stopped her | Blunt, hospitable, unsentimental | Unchanged; she is the weather they pass through | Commands, then one long sentence that matters | Boiler suit, wellingtons, a collie at heel
~ callum - priya | best friends | she navigates; he drives | she walks ahead; then she pushes
~ callum - archie | grandson and grandfather, as he thought | a stolen float and a head start | a harbour wall, an adaptor, and no asking
~ callum - eilidh | son and the mother who wrote | a photograph and an address | one afternoon and a letter each
~ archie - eilidh | the man who kept her letters and the girl who sent them | fifteen years of silence | he waits on the esplanade and does not come to the door
# First Charge | INT | Reid's Flat, Kitchen | DAY | callum, archie | Find the tin | {archie}'s retirement clock on the table. {callum}, looking for the float's logbook, finds a biscuit tin of letters addressed to him in a hand he doesn't know | {callum} learns he is adopted | 5 | props=biscuit tin+letters+photograph
# First Charge | EXT | Dairy Depot, Yard | DAWN | callum, priya | Take the float | Four a.m. He unplugs it; she is already in the passenger seat with the map. He asks how she knew. She says he is not complicated | They leave | 4 | vehicles=milk float, props=map+milk crates
# First Charge | EXT | A82, Loch Lomond | DAY | callum, priya | Be slow in public | Rain, eight miles an hour, eleven caravans behind them. {priya} waves them past like royalty. He laughs for the first time since the tin | | 4 | vehicles=milk float+caravans+cars
# Second Charge | INT | Lamont's Farm, Milking Parlour | DAWN | callum, morag, priya | Pay for the electricity | They milk for their charge. {morag}, between cows, says she gave a boy up in 1957 and what she would want him to know, and goes back to the cluster | {callum} hears the other side before he arrives | 5 | animals=dairy cows+collie, props=milking clusters
# Second Charge | INT | Inveraray, Village Hall | NIGHT | callum, priya | Lose the summer | A ceilidh. She dances a Strip The Willow with a local boy and comes back shining. He is foul to her and doesn't know why | | 5 | dance, crowd=60, props=fiddles+accordion
# Third Charge | EXT | Rest And Be Thankful | DAY | callum, priya | Hear it | Three miles an hour up the pass. She tells him: Leicester, September. He says she is just like everyone, leaving him out. She gets out and walks, and is faster than the float | {priya} tells him; he fails it | 6 | vehicles=milk float
# Third Charge | EXT | Inveraray, Harbour Wall | DUSK | callum, archie | Say why | {archie}, off the bus with a carrier bag. He kept the letters because he couldn't answer and couldn't burn them. He gives {callum} the adaptor he forgot, and does not ask him home | {archie} lets him go on | 7 | props=carrier bag+charger adaptor
# Fourth Charge | EXT | Oban, Guest House Door | DAY | callum, eilidh | Arrive | She opens the door with a tea towel, sees the float, reads the dairy's name off its side and sits down on her own step | {callum} meets his mother | 4 | vehicles=milk float
# Fourth Charge | INT | Oban, Guest House Landing | DAY | callum, eilidh | Have the afternoon | She shows him how to bleed a radiator. He gives her his letter. The hall clock says half past four; her daughters are due at five. He says he should go, and means it | {callum} chooses to leave | 6 | props=radiator key+letter
# Fourth Charge | EXT | Oban, Esplanade | DUSK | callum, priya, archie | Go home | The float dies in sight of the ferry. {archie} and {priya} get behind it. {callum} steers. Nobody says anything about September | | 3 | vehicles=milk float, props=ferry in background
